"Spherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31640view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"lineno\":122}); Total lines: 533\nL96: While additive baselines frequently degrade open-ended generation—evidenced by drops in the TRUE$\\times$INFO metric—Spherical Steering simultaneously boosts multiple-choice accuracy (by up to $15\\%$) and open-ended quality. These results validate our core hypothesis: by respecting the hyperspherical geometry of representations, we achieve robust control that sharpens reasoning without shattering the manifold required for coherent generation.\nL97: Our main contributions are:\nL98: \nL99:   * •\nL100: \nL101: We propose Spherical Steering, a training-free intervention that controls model behavior via activation rotation and vMF confidence gating, providing a geometry-consistent alternative to additive shifts.\nL102: \nL103:   * •\nL104: \nL105: Experiments on Llama-3.1 and Qwen-2.5 show our method push the accuracy-generation frontier: it achieves state-of-the-art multiple-choice accuracy (improving up to 15%) while simultaneously enhancing open-ended generation quality.\nL106: \nL107:   * •\nL108: We demonstrate via effective-rank analysis that spherical rotation is significantly more collapse-efficient than additive steering, enabling robust control without degrading the representation manifold.\nL109: ## 2 Related Work\nL110: ##### Activation Addition and Inference-time Intervention.\nL111: Activation steering modifies hidden activations at inference time to control LLM behavior (cite75†Zou et al., 2023 ; cite76†Hernandez et al., 2023 ; cite77†Leong et al., 2023 ; cite78†Turner et al., 2024 ). Many methods construct steering directions from supervision signals, including probe-based approaches (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ; cite94†Chen et al., 2024 ) and contrastive averaging such as CAA and ORTHO (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 ; cite95†Arditi et al., 2024 ).\nL112: Others exploit structure in representation space, e.g., spectral projections into behavior-aligned subspaces (cite96†Qiu et al., 2024 ) or interpretable latent features from sparse autoencoders (SAEs) (cite97†Hernandez et al., 2021 ; cite98†Cunningham et al., 2023 ; cite99†Gao et al., 2024 ; cite100†Marks et al., 2024 ). Beyond static edits, recent work highlights the importance of more flexible inference-time mechanisms (cite101†Cheng et al., 2025 ; cite102†Wang et al., 2024 ; cite103†Wang et al., 2025 ). cite104†Grant et al.\nL113: (2025) points out that current representation editing methods often diverge from the normal representation distribution, making the model easily collapse and degrading its utility. Complementary to additive edits, Angular Steering (cite105†Vu & Nguyen, 2025 ) rotates activations after projecting them onto a fixed 2-dimension steering plane, demonstrating angle-based control for safety refusal and emotion modulation.\nL114: In contrast, we rotate directly in the original space via hyperspherical geodesics with norm preservation without relying on a low-dimensional projection or PCA assumptions, and use confidence gating for input-adaptive intervention strength.\nL115: cite106†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of Spherical Steering. Contrastive pairs define a truthfulness axis $(\\mu_{T},\\mu_{H})$. We steer by a norm-preserving geodesic rotation of hidden activations toward $\\mu_{T}$, and use a vMF confidence gate to selectively apply steering with input-adaptive strength $t$.\nL116: ##### Learnable Interventions and Representation Fine-tuning.\nL117: Representation fine-tuning learns structured latent interventions via lightweight modules, operating in representation space instead of updating all model parameters. Early frameworks such as ReFT (cite84†Wu et al., 2024 ) and LoFiT (cite107†Yin et al., 2024 ) focus on identifying orthogonal control properties or localized editing mechanisms (cite108†Meng et al., 2022a ; cite109†Stolfo et al., 2023 ).\nL118: Building on these foundations, recent methods improve the utility and stability of learned interventions by jointly optimizing where to intervene and how the intervention is parameterized (cite110†Deng et al., 2025a ; cite111†Wu et al., 2025 ). JoLA (cite112†Lai et al., 2025 ) further learns sparse, gated head edits with mixed edit forms. HPR (cite113†Pham & Nguyen, 2024 ) also adopts a direction–magnitude view and enforces norm preservation via Householder reflection.\nL119: It learns a separating hyperplane to identify undesirable states, and further trains an angle-prediction module to perform input-adaptive geometric updates. Unlike HPR, our method is fully training-free and uses a closed-form geodesic rotation toward a contrastive prototype direction, with a lightweight distribution-based confidence gate for selective and controllable intervention.\nL120: ## 3 Method\nL121: \nL122: As shown in Figure cite114†2 , we introduce Spherical Steering, a novel activation steering method that brings geometry-aware steering to hidden representations by operating on their hyperspherical structure. In the following subsections, we will describe the prototype construction, addition vs. rotation and adaptive gating in detail.\nL123: ### 3.1 Prototype Construction\nL124: \nL125: Consider a language model $\\mathcal{M}$ and a contrastive dataset $\\mathcal{D}=\\{(x_{i},y_{i}^{+},y_{i}^{-})\\}_{i=1}^{N}$, where $x_{i}$ is an input question and $y_{i}^{+}/y_{i}^{-}$ are its corresponding positive and negative answers. We extract training-free activations from $\\mathcal{M}$ and aggregate them into a single discriminative prototype direction.\nL126: ##### Offline Representation Extraction.\nL127: \nL128: For each instance $(x_{i},y_{i}^{+},y_{i}^{-})$, we forward the concatenated sequences $x_{i}\\|y_{i}^{+}$ and $x_{i}\\|y_{i}^{-}$ through $\\mathcal{M}$, where $\\|$ denotes concatenation of the question and answer. We then extract the layer-$l$ activation of the last token, which typically summarizes the semantics of the full sequence. We denote the extracted last-token activations of layer-$l$ as\nL129:  | $$z_{i}^{(l)+}=h^{(l)}_{\\text{last}}(x_{i}\\|y_{i}^{+}),\\qquad z_{i}^{(l)-}=h^{(l)}_{\\text{last}}(x_{i}\\|y_{i}^{-}).$$  |  | (1)\nL130: \nL131: The $h^{(l)}_{\\text{last}}(\\cdot)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ where d is the hidden dimension of language model $\\mathcal{M}$. This step is performed once offline and keeps model $\\mathcal{M}$ fixed.\nL132: ##### Prototype Construction via Contrastive Mean.\nL133: \nL134: We aggregate the extracted features by computing the mean representation for positive and negative answers:\nL135: \nL136:  | $$m_{+}^{(l)}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{(l)+},\\qquad m_{-}^{(l)}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}^{(l)-}.$$  |  | (2)\nL137: \nL138: We then form a discriminative direction by taking their difference (also in Figure cite114†2 leftmost),\nL139: \nL140:  | $$\\Delta^{(l)}=m_{+}^{(l)}-m_{-}^{(l)},$$  |  | (3)\nL141: which isolates components that consistently distinguish positive from negative outputs while suppressing shared context. Finally, we normalize to obtain a unit prototype direction:\nL142: \nL143:  | $$\\mu^{(l)}=\\frac{\\Delta^{(l)}}{\\|\\Delta^{(l)}\\|},$$  |  | (4)\nL144: \nL145: which denote direction that summarizes the contrastive signal at layer $l$. For readability, we drop the layer superscript when $l$ is clear and write $\\mu=\\mu^{(l)}$.\nL146: ### 3.2 Norm-preserving Steering Rotation\nL147: ##### Activation Addition and Limitation.\nL148: \nL149: Given the prototype direction $\\mu$ constructed in §cite11†3.1 , at inference time we let $h^{(l)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ denote the token activation at layer $l$. The conventional activation addition approach applies a linear intervention:\nL150: \nL151:  | $$h^{(l)\\prime}=h^{(l)}+\\lambda\\,\\mu,\\quad\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$$  |  | (5)\nL152: \nL153: where $\\lambda$ controls intervention strength.\nL154: \nL155: The squared norm after intervention becomes:\nL156:  | $\\displaystyle\\|h^{(l)\\prime}\\|^{2}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\|h^{(l)}\\|^{2}+2\\lambda\\,\\mu^{\\top}h^{(l)}+\\lambda^{2}\\|\\mu\\|^{2}$  |  | (6)\nL157:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\|h^{(l)}\\|^{2}+2\\lambda\\,\\mu^{\\top}h^{(l)}+\\lambda^{2},$  |\nL158: \nL159: yielding a relative norm change:\nL160: \nL161:  | $$\\frac{\\|h^{(l)\\prime}\\|}{\\|h^{(l)}\\|}=\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2\\lambda\\,\\mu^{\\top}h^{(l)}+\\lambda^{2}}{\\|h^{(l)}\\|^{2}}}.$$  |  | (7)\nL162: This introduces uncontrolled magnitude distortion varying with both $\\lambda$ and alignment $\\mu^{\\top}h^{(l)}$, disrupting the architectural prior of normalization layers that stabilize magnitude while encoding semantics in direction.\nL163: ##### Activation Rotation.\nL164: \nL165: In this work, we instead steer by rotation on the hypersphere $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}=\\{u\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}:\\|u\\|=1\\}$, rather than an unconstrained shift in $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$.\nL166: \nL167: To guide this rotation, we first define a two-sided truthfulness axis on the hypersphere:\nL168: \nL169:  | $$\\mu_{T}=\\mu,\\qquad\\mu_{H}=-\\mu_{T},$$  |  | (8)\nL170: where $\\mu_{T}$ is the Truthful direction and $\\mu_{H}$ is the antipodal hallucinated direction. Our steering rotates the activation toward $\\mu_{T}$ while preserving its magnitude.\nL171: \nL172: Let $\\hat{h}^{(l)}=h^{(l)}/\\|h^{(l)}\\|\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ denote the normalized activation direction. We measure the angular distance to the truthful direction as\nL173: \nL174:  | $$\\theta\\;=\\;\\arccos\\!\\big(\\mu_{T}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)}\\big)\\in[0,\\pi].$$  |  | (9)\nL175: We parameterize the amount of steering by a scalar $t\\in[0,1]$, where $t=0$ leaves the state unchanged and $t=1$ rotates the direction fully to $\\mu_{T}$. The updated direction is obtained by moving along the shortest great-circle path between $\\hat{h}^{(l)}$ and $\\mu_{T}$, implemented via spherical linear interpolation (Slerp) (cite115†Shoemake, 1985 ):\nL176: \nL177:  | $$\\hat{h}^{(l)\\prime}=\\frac{\\sin((1-t)\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}\\,\\hat{h}^{(l)}+\\frac{\\sin(t\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}\\,\\mu_{T},\\quad(\\theta\\neq 0),$$  |  | (10)\nL178: and we set $\\hat{h}^{(l)\\prime}=\\hat{h}^{(l)}$ when $\\theta=0$. Finally, we restore the original magnitude to obtain the intervened activation\nL179: \nL180:  | $$h^{(l)\\prime}\\;=\\;\\|h^{(l)}\\|\\,\\hat{h}^{(l)\\prime}.$$  |  | (11)\nL181: By construction, $\\|h^{(l)\\prime}\\|=\\|h^{(l)}\\|$; thus, this update is non-additive and strictly norm-preserving. Moreover, it follows the geodesic from $\\hat{h}^{(l)}$ to $\\mu_{T}$, yielding a minimal angular change on $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ for a given step size $t$. In contrast to activation addition, which shifts representations by a fixed offset in $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, our intervention changes the activation through a geometry-aware rotation on the sphere.\nL182: ### 3.3 vMF Confidence Gating on the Hypersphere\nL183: \nL184: To make steering more selective and controllable, we further define a confidence-based gate that determines the steering strength $t$ from the current activation direction.\nL185: \nL186: Given the current activation direction $\\hat{h}^{(l)}$ (defined in §cite14†3.2 ), we measure its alignment to the Truthful and hallucinated directions by cosine similarity:\nL187: \nL188:  | $$s_{T}=\\mu_{T}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)},\\qquad s_{H}=\\mu_{H}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)}.$$  |  | (12)\nL189: To map these alignments into a calibrated confidence signal, we use the exponential form induced by the von Mises–Fisher (vMF) distribution (cite90†Mardia & Jupp, 2009 ). For a unit direction $u\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ with mean direction $m\\in\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$, the vMF density has the form $f(u;m,\\kappa)\\propto\\exp(\\kappa\\,m^{\\top}u)$, where $\\kappa>0$ controls concentration around $m$.\nL190: Since we only require relative evidence between the two antipodal directions, we treat the exponential term as a prototype score and normalize with a two-class softmax:\nL191:  | $\\displaystyle\\displaystyle p_{T}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{e^{\\kappa s_{T}}}{e^{\\kappa s_{T}}+e^{\\kappa s_{H}}},$  |  | (13)\nL192:  | $\\displaystyle\\displaystyle p_{H}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{e^{\\kappa s_{H}}}{e^{\\kappa s_{T}}+e^{\\kappa s_{H}}}.$  |\nL193: \nL194: We summarize the tendency toward the hallucinated direction by:\nL195: \nL196:  | $$\\delta=p_{H}-p_{T}\\in[-1,1].$$  |  | (14)\nL197: \nL198: We then map this confidence score to an intervention strength $t\\in[0,1]$ via a thresholded, bounded rule:\nL199:  | $$t=\\begin{cases}0,&\\delta\\leq\\beta,\\\\[8.0pt]\nL200: \\displaystyle\\mathrm{clip}\\!\\left(\\alpha\\cdot\\frac{\\delta-\\beta}{1-\\beta},\\,0,\\,1\\right),&\\delta>\\beta,\\end{cases}$$  |  | (15)\nL201: where $\\beta\\in[-1,1)$ controls steering conservativeness, $\\alpha\\in(0,1]$ scales the rotation (and thus the maximum adjustment), and $\\kappa$ controls how sharply the confidence changes with alignment (as induced by the vMF concentration parameter). In summary, the gate computes $t$ from $\\hat{h}^{(l)}$, yielding $t=0$ when steering is unnecessary and larger $t$ when the activation is more aligned with the hallucinated direction.\nL202: ### 3.4 Inference-Time Procedure\nL203: \nL204: We now describe the complete inference procedure that integrates the components from §cite11†3.1 –§cite17†3.3 .\nL205: ##### Generation Procedure.\nL206: \nL207: Given an input prompt and the selected steering layers $\\mathcal{L}=\\{l_{1},\\ldots,l_{K}\\}$ with their corresponding prototypes $\\{\\mu_{T}^{(l)}\\}_{l\\in\\mathcal{L}}$, we generate tokens autoregressively. At each decoding step $j$, for each layer $l\\in\\mathcal{L}$:\nL208: \nL209:   * •\nL210: \nL211: Extract the current token’s activation $h^{(l)}_{j}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and normalize: $\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j}=h^{(l)}_{j}/\\|h^{(l)}_{j}\\|$.\nL212: \nL213:   * •\nL214: Compute alignment scores: $s_{T}=\\mu_{T}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j}$ and $s_{H}=\\mu_{H}^{\\top}\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j}$ respectively.\nL215: \nL216:   * •\nL217: \nL218: Apply vMF gate (Eq. cite116†15 ) to obtain steering strength $t_{j}$.\nL219: \nL220:   * •\nL221: \nL222: If $t_{j}>0$, rotate via Slerp and restore magnitude: $h^{(l)\\prime}_{j}=\\|h^{(l)}_{j}\\|\\cdot\\text{Slerp}(\\hat{h}^{(l)}_{j},\\mu_{T},t_{j})$; otherwise keep $h^{(l)\\prime}_{j}=h^{(l)}_{j}$.\nL223: The intervened activation $h^{(l)\\prime}_{j}$ is then passed to subsequent layers. The confidence gate enables input-adaptive steering where $t_{j}$ varies across tokens based on their directional alignment, automatically reducing intervention when the model is already confident and truthful.\nL224: ## 4 Experiments\nL225: We present a comprehensive empirical evaluation of Spherical Steering across diverse reasoning benchmarks.\nL226: Our experiments reveal three key findings: (1) Spherical Steering achieves state-of-the-art multiple-choice performance, surpassing activation addition baselines by substantial margins; (2) unlike additive methods that degrade generation quality, our approach simultaneously improves both accuracy and fluency; and (3) norm-preserving rotation yields superior collapse-efficiency, achieving stronger performance gains with less representational degradation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent Chain-of-Thought (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08220v1)\nciteturn31640view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view1\",\"lineno\":82}); Total lines: 343\nL67: Experiments with Llama architectures show that adaptive latent CoT consistently improves language modeling perplexity and broad downstream accuracy, even with fewer training FLOPs than prior recurrent baselines.\nL68: ## 1 Introduction\nL69: The remarkable success of Large Language Models (LLMs) has been primarily driven by scaling up model parameters and training data (cite46†Kaplan et al., 2020 ; cite47†Liu et al., 2024 ; cite48†Brown et al., 2020 ).\nL70: However, this paradigm is approaching critical bottlenecks: the exhaustion of high-quality public data (cite49†Muennighoff et al., 2023 ; cite50†Villalobos et al., 2022 ) and the substantial communication overheads associated with scaling model size (cite51†Pati et al., 2023 ; cite52†Narayanan et al., 2021 ; cite53†Hoffmann et al., 2022 ). Consequently, a key research direction is to improve model capability under a fixed parameter and data budget by scaling compute per token.\nL71: To scale compute without expanding parameters, prior work has explored parameter-sharing strategies that emulate greater depth by recursively reusing the same layer weights (cite54†Zeng et al., 2025b ; cite55†Giannou et al., 2023 ; cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ). While appealing, this form of depth scaling can be prone to training instability.\nL72: More recently, PonderLM2 (cite57†Zeng et al., 2025a ) introduces latent computation before predicting the next token, but parallelizing this process often requires Jacobi-style iteration and incurs substantial training FLOPs. Moreover, these approaches typically allocate uniform compute to all tokens, which is inherently suboptimal compared to human cognition where thinking is adaptive—brief for simple concepts and prolonged for complex reasoning.\nL73: Parallel to architectural modifications, another line of research has demonstrated that scaling inference-time compute—via CoT (cite58†Wei et al., 2022 )—has achieved remarkable success. However, this capability typically relies on explicit supervision (annotated CoT data), is confined to a discrete token space, and is ultimately capped by the base model’s pre-trained capabilities (cite59†Yue et al., 2025 ).\nL74: These observations motivate a fundamental question: Can we internalize the benefits of Chain-of-Thought directly into pre-training, within a continuous latent space, and learn to allocate compute adaptively? In this work, we propose Pretraining LLMs with Adaptive Latent CoT, a pre-training framework that encourages the model to generate an adaptive sequence of latent CoT steps before emitting each observed token.\nL75: The model learns to produce short (or even zero) latent trajectories for simple tokens while allocating longer latent chains for complex concepts.\nL76: Unlike prior methods that require multi-stage training (cite60†Fu et al., 2025 ; cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite61†Schuster et al., 2022 ), additional supervision (cite62†Elbayad et al., 2019 ), only reduce inference compute (cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite61†Schuster et al., 2022 ; cite63†Banino et al., 2021 ), or assume a prescribed halting prior (cite64†Chen et al., 2025 ; cite65†Raposo et al., 2024 ; cite66†Bae et al., 2025 ; cite63†Banino et al., 2021 ; cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ), our adaptive latent CoT emerges naturally from one-stage pre-training on general text, and can reduce computation in both training and inference.\nL77: ## 2 Related works\nL78: ##### Latent Reasoning\nL79: To improve model capability without increasing parameters, an emerging line of work studies latent reasoning, where LLMs conduct reasoning internally in hidden states rather than via explicit verbalization. One branch remains in the discrete vocabulary space by inserting specialized markers—e.g., Pause (cite67†Goyal et al., 2023 ), Filler (cite68†Pfau et al., 2024 ), or Planning (cite69†Wang et al., 2023 ) tokens—to allocate additional “thinking time” and induce extra implicit computation steps.\nL80: Similarly, Quiet-STaR (cite70†Zelikman et al., 2024 ) uses reinforcement learning to generate token-level rationales. However, these approaches are fundamentally constrained by the discreteness of the vocabulary, which limits the expressiveness and flexibility of the reasoning process. Consequently, another line of work moves reasoning into continuous spaces.\nL81: For example, Coconut and SIM-CoT (cite71†Hao et al., 2024 ; cite72†Wei et al., 2025 ) leverage hidden states (e.g., the final-layer representations) as continuous inputs to simulate chain-of-thought. Despite encouraging results, most existing continuous latent reasoning methods still rely on supervised fine-tuning and curated CoT annotations, which hinders scalability.\nL82: ##### Recursive Models\nL83: In parallel, an orthogonal way to improve capability under a fixed parameter budget is recursive parameter sharing, which scales compute per token by iteratively reusing the same weights. Universal Transformers (cite73†Dehghani et al., 2018 ) show that weight sharing can provide strong capacity with minimal parameter growth, and ALBERT (cite74†Lan et al., 2019 ) further improves efficiency via extensive sharing without hurting performance.\nL84: More recent work leverages recurrence to scale effective depth and performance: PonderLM2 (cite57†Zeng et al., 2025a ) adds recursive latent computation before next-token prediction and reports strong gains, though parallelizing often relies on Jacobi-style iteration and can be FLOP-heavy.\nL85: Looped Transformers, PonderLM, and related methods (cite54†Zeng et al., 2025b ; cite55†Giannou et al., 2023 ; cite75†Geiping et al., 2025 ) similarly reuse parameters to increase depth and show benefits on general modeling and reasoning tasks. However, as effective depth grows, training can become unstable, and compute is uniform across tokens regardless of input difficulty.\nL86: ##### Adaptive Computation\nL87: Many works adopt Adaptive Computation Time (ACT) (cite76†Graves, 2016 ) to let models decide per token how many iterative steps to run, and this has inspired a broad class of adaptive token/layer routing methods. For example, ITT (cite64†Chen et al., 2025 ) uses a predefined per-layer token-selection ratio, while MoD/MoR (cite65†Raposo et al., 2024 ; cite66†Bae et al., 2025 ) activate only top-$k$ tokens. PonderNet (cite63†Banino et al., 2021 ) adds a geometric halting prior to stabilize learning.\nL88: However, these approaches typically fix or strongly constrain compute allocation a priori, limiting the model’s ability to explore adaptive policies during training. A complementary line achieves adaptivity via multi-stage training and inference-time early exiting, e.g., TaH (cite60†Fu et al., 2025 ), Ouro (cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ), and CALM (cite61†Schuster et al., 2022 ).\nL89: Ouro, for instance, learns an exit gate with an entropy regularizer during pretraining and then freezes the LM while fine-tuning only the gate. Other methods use extra supervision, such as Depth-adaptive Transformers (cite62†Elbayad et al., 2019 ) that predict when to skip remaining layers using likelihood/correctness signals.\nL90: Moreover, methods like Ouro, PonderNet, and CALM (cite56†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite63†Banino et al., 2021 ; cite61†Schuster et al., 2022 ) typically still require full computation during training, so they do not reduce training FLOPs and mainly save compute at inference.\nL91: In contrast, we propose a one-stage pretraining framework that learns ACT end-to-end without a priori constraints, reducing computation in both training and inference.\nL92: ## 3 Method\nL93: We propose an efficient latent Chain-of-Thought (latent-CoT) training and inference framework that removes the sequential dependency bottleneck while attaining adaptive compute.\nL94: Our method consists of three key components: (1) Parallel Masking that extends attention causality to a 2D index $(t,k)$ (token position and latent step), enabling parallel computation over all positions at each latent step; (2) a probabilistic halting mechanism with a lightweight Router that models continuation/exit probabilities, together with threshold pruning and mass-preserving mixing to obtain the final representation; and (3) a correctness-aware adaptive loss that discourages unnecessary latent computation when the model is already confident on the ground-truth token.\nL95: ### 3.1 The Sequential Dependency Bottleneck\nL96: Let $\\mathbf{x}=\\{x_{1},\\dots,x_{L}\\}$ denote the input sequence. In a standard latent CoT framework, for each position $t$ the model inserts a sequence of latent steps $\\mathbf{z}_{t}=\\{z_{t}^{(1)},\\dots,z_{t}^{(K)}\\}$ before the subsequent target token $x_{t+1}$. Here, $z_{t}^{(k)}$ denotes the last-layer hidden state at position $t$ produced by the $k$-th forward pass.\nL97: The model generates these latent-step representations autoregressively, which introduces a strict sequential dependency across both the sequence dimension ($t$) and the latent-step dimension ($k$).\nL98: cite77†Image: Refer to caption L99: Figure 1: Left: Standard latent-CoT induces a strict sequential chain across both sequence length $L$ and latent depth $K$ (highlighted in red): later tokens (e.g., $x_{2}$) can depend on deeper latent states of earlier positions.\nL100: Right: Enforcing strict 2D causality with an attention mask that allows $(t_{i},k_{i})\\!\\rightarrow\\!(t_{j},k_{j})$ only if $t_{j}\\leq t_{i}$ and $k_{j}\\leq k_{i}$ blocks cross-token dependencies on future latent steps (shown by $\\times$), making the process sequential only in $k$ and enabling parallel computation over all $t$ at each step.\nL101: ##### A minimal example.\nL102: \nL103: Consider autoregressive generation for tokens $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ with latent steps. The hidden state $\\mathbf{h}_{x_{2}}$ for $x_{2}$ depends on the complete resolution of all latent steps associated with $x_{1}$:\nL104: \nL105:  | $$\\mathbf{h}_{x_{2}}=\\text{LM}\\!\\left(x_{1},z_{1}^{(1)},\\dots,z_{1}^{(K)},x_{2}\\right).$$  |  | (1)\nL106: As illustrated in cite78†Figure 1 (left), this means $\\mathbf{h}_{x_{2}}$ cannot be computed until the entire chain $z_{1}^{(1\\dots K)}$ is finalized, since attention requires access to progressively deeper latent hidden states (highlighted by the red dependencies).\nL107: ##### The pre-training dilemma.\nL108: Naively training under this strictly sequential dependency incurs $O(L\\times K)$ sequential operations per sequence, which becomes computationally prohibitive for long contexts (e.g., thousands of tokens). PonderLM2 (cite57†Zeng et al., 2025a ) mitigates this by computing latent states for every token at each latent step simultaneously and then refining them with multi-step Jacobi iterations.\nL109: While this enables parallelism, the iterative refinement substantially increases training compute—for example, even with a latent step budget of $K=1$, it can incur around $8\\times$ the training FLOPs of the vanilla model.\nL110: ### 3.2 Parallel Latent Steps via Parallel Masking\nL111: \nL112: To enable parallel computation while maintaining training efficiency, we expand attention into two dimensions: sequence position ($t$) and latent step ($k$). We define a Parallel Attention Mask over 2D indices $(t,k)$ that enforces strict causality in both dimensions:\nL113: \nL114:  | $$M_{(t_{i},k_{i}),(t_{j},k_{j})}=\\begin{cases}0&\\text{if }t_{j}\\leq t_{i}\\ \\land\\ k_{j}\\leq k_{i},\\\\\nL115: -\\infty&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL116: This mask induces a partial order over the 2D computation graph (cite78†Figure 1 , right): at any fixed latent step $k$, the model can compute states for the entire sequence $t\\in[1,L]$ in parallel. Unlike standard autoregressive generation which is sequential in $L$, our training process effectively unrolls the latent iterations.\nL117: This transforms the dependency graph from $O(L\\times K)$ sequential operations to $O(K)$ sequential steps, thereby leveraging the massive parallelism of GPUs over the sequence dimension.\nL118: ### 3.3 Adaptive Computation via Probabilistic Halting\nL119: While the parallel masking enables efficient unrolling over latent steps, executing many steps uniformly for all tokens is not always desirable. Empirically, once a token is already likely correct (high $p_{\\mathrm{target}}$), further latent computation yields diminishing returns and can even hurt the target probability cite79†Figure 2 . We therefore introduce a probabilistic halting mechanism to adapt computation per token.\nL120: We denote the maximum latent CoT length by $\\ell_{\\max}$, and implement it with $K_{\\max}=\\ell_{\\max}+1$.\nL121: cite80†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Motivation for adaptive computation and the correctness-aware adaptive loss. We probe a latent-CoT model without adaptive computation on 0.34B tokens. At each latent step $k$, we bucket tokens by their current target-token probability $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ (x-axis), and measure the average improvement in the target probability brought by the next latent step, $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k+1)}-p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ (y-axis).\nL122: While extra latent computation substantially improves low-confidence tokens, the gains diminish as $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ increases, and can become negative for already confident tokens (negative bars), suggesting unnecessary or even harmful computation.\nL123: ##### Router (conditional continuation).\nL124: \nL125: At each latent step $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,K_{\\max}\\}$, the Router predicts the conditional probability of continuing to the next latent step:\nL126: \nL127:  | $$g_{t}^{(k)}\\triangleq P(\\text{Continue}\\mid\\text{Reach step }k)=\\sigma\\!\\bigl(\\mathrm{Linear}(z_{t}^{(k)})\\bigr).$$  |  | (3)\nL128: \nL129: Accordingly, $P(\\text{Stop}\\mid\\text{Reach step }k)=1-g_{t}^{(k)}$.\nL130: ##### Reach probability (accumulated probability flow).\nL131: \nL132: For computation to exist at step $k$, it must have continued through all previous steps. We define the reach probability recursively:\nL133: \nL134:  | $$p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(1)}\\triangleq 1,\\qquad p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}\\triangleq p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k)}\\,g_{t}^{(k)}.$$  |  | (4)\nL135: ##### Exit probability (halting distribution).\nL136: \nL137: The probability of halting exactly at step $k$ is the joint probability of reaching step $k$ and deciding to stop:\nL138: \nL139:  | $$p_{\\mathrm{exit},t}^{(k)}\\triangleq P(K_{t}=k)=p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k)}\\bigl(1-g_{t}^{(k)}\\bigr).$$  |  | (5)\nL140: \n"
